WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.580
What does Finpoint do?

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.111
The problem

00:00:03.411 --> 00:00:07.042
Your bank shows that money moved, but not what it was really for.

00:00:07.222 --> 00:00:10.698
One supermarket payment might be groceries and a gift.

00:00:10.878 --> 00:00:13.795
One dinner payment might include your friends’ shares.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:15.588
The idea

00:00:15.888 --> 00:00:23.078
Finpoint links each bank amount to your own explanations, then turns those explanations into spending and plans.

00:00:23.258 --> 00:00:25.454
You only need one purchase to start.

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:27.266
Step 1

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:29.356
Try the sample lunch lesson.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:31.186
Step 2

00:00:31.486 --> 00:00:34.519
Import a bank CSV, or restore a backup.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.372
Step 3

00:00:36.672 --> 00:00:38.222
Explain one bank row.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.079
Step 4

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:41.668
Download a backup.

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:43.697
Check your result

00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:47.293
You can say where one bank amount went, and find it again.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:49.125
Watch out

00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:51.850
An empty Dashboard is normal at first.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:55.394
Imported bank rows don’t count until you explain them.

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:57.126
Example

00:00:57.426 --> 00:00:59.575
Alex paid $12 for lunch.

00:00:59.755 --> 00:01:01.915
The bank shows −$12.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:08.575
Alex adds a Lunch Record for −$12, and an Allocation of −$12 connects them.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.514
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:14.045
The problem

00:01:14.345 --> 00:01:18.739
Bank rows and real-life purchases don’t always match one for one.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.522
The idea

00:01:20.822 --> 00:01:23.109
A Statement is one row from your bank.

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:26.017
A Record is your explanation of what happened.

00:01:26.197 --> 00:01:29.789
An Allocation links an amount from a Statement to a Record.

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:32.718
An Account is where money sits, not your login.

00:01:32.898 --> 00:01:36.437
Money paid is negative, and money received is positive.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.233
Step 1

00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:40.910
Read the Statement’s amount and description.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:42.720
Step 2

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.750
Decide what it really was.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.572
Step 3

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:48.873
Create a Record that explains it.

00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:50.712
Step 4

00:01:51.012 --> 00:01:52.285
Allocate the amount.

00:01:52.465 --> 00:01:57.681
One Statement can feed several Records, and one Record can combine several Statements.

00:01:58.533 --> 00:01:59.697
Check your result

00:01:59.997 --> 00:02:07.582
A Record is complete when it has an Allocation, its Allocation total equals its amount, and none of its Statements are Pending.

00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:09.392
Watch out

00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:12.547
Two bank rows don’t always mean two Records.

00:02:12.727 --> 00:02:16.527
A payment and its repayment can explain one shared purchase.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.260
Example

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.057
Statement −$12, Allocation −$12, Lunch Record −$12.

00:02:25.237 --> 00:02:29.294
Your Account balance and your Dashboard spending answer different questions.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.700
Starting fresh, or restoring

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.231
The problem

00:02:34.531 --> 00:02:39.974
A new workspace comes with starter Categories and spending buckets, but no bank activity yet.

00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:41.755
The idea

00:02:42.055 --> 00:02:45.120
Everything is stored in this browser, on this device.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.640
The starter defaults are yours to edit.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.361
You can learn with practice lessons first, or restore a backup you already have.

00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:54.166
Step 1

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:55.307
New here?

00:02:55.487 --> 00:02:57.498
Open Importer and choose your bank.

00:02:58.367 --> 00:02:59.320
Step 2

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:00.681
Have a backup?

00:03:00.861 --> 00:03:02.584
Open Sync and restore it.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:04.439
Step 3

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:07.850
Export your current data first if you want to keep it.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.629
Restoring replaces it;

00:03:09.809 --> 00:03:10.918
it doesn’t merge.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:12.779
Step 4

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:16.723
Use Help in the top bar any time, without leaving a form.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.764
Check your result

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:23.086
Your Accounts, Statements or restored Records show up in their lists.

00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:24.925
Watch out

00:03:25.225 --> 00:03:27.737
Load demo data is not a tutorial.

00:03:27.917 --> 00:03:29.424
It replaces your data.

00:03:29.604 --> 00:03:31.633
Use the practice lessons instead.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.360
Example

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:41.202
In practice, you can explain a −$12 lunch without uploading anything or changing your real Account balance.

00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:44.453
Importing bank activity

00:03:45.053 --> 00:03:45.984
The problem

00:03:46.284 --> 00:03:50.172
Finpoint can only read a bank file in a format it supports.

00:03:50.352 --> 00:03:52.652
Each row becomes one of your Statements.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:54.455
The idea

00:03:54.755 --> 00:04:01.135
Importer supports CSV exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut.

00:04:01.315 --> 00:04:03.901
It creates Statements, not finished Records.

00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:06.602
Missing Accounts are created as you go.

00:04:06.782 --> 00:04:11.234
Revolut takes one file at a time, and asks which Account it belongs to.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.033
Step 1

00:04:13.333 --> 00:04:15.305
Export a CSV from your bank.

00:04:15.485 --> 00:04:16.982
Don’t edit its columns.

00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:18.786
Step 2

00:04:19.086 --> 00:04:21.962
Open Importer, pick your bank, and choose the file.

00:04:22.833 --> 00:04:23.805
Step 3

00:04:24.105 --> 00:04:28.365
Import, then read the inserted, re-indexed and unchanged counts.

00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:30.212
Step 4

00:04:30.512 --> 00:04:32.456
Check the new rows in Statements.

00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:34.497
Check your result

00:04:34.797 --> 00:04:38.264
The rows you expected are there, with the right dates and signs.

00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:40.092
Watch out

00:04:40.392 --> 00:04:43.744
If an import fails, check the bank you picked and the file.

00:04:43.924 --> 00:04:46.881
A PDF or a renamed spreadsheet won’t work.

00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:48.593
Example

00:04:48.893 --> 00:04:54.422
Alex imports a salary of +$3,000 and a lunch of −$12.

00:04:54.602 --> 00:04:58.084
Neither one is explained until Alex creates Records.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.464
Explaining your first purchase

00:05:02.064 --> 00:05:02.995
The problem

00:05:03.295 --> 00:05:09.153
The lunch Statement says −$12, but the Dashboard only counts what a Record explains.

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:10.955
The idea

00:05:11.255 --> 00:05:14.371
Allocator lists the bank amounts you haven’t explained yet.

00:05:14.551 --> 00:05:18.592
Pick one and create a Record with a title, an amount and a Category.

00:05:18.772 --> 00:05:21.288
Finpoint suggests the Allocation for you.

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.099
Step 1

00:05:23.399 --> 00:05:26.026
Open Allocator and pick the lunch Statement.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:27.853
Step 2

00:05:28.153 --> 00:05:29.871
Create a Record called Lunch:

00:05:30.051 --> 00:05:32.420
Paid $12, in a food Category.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.272
Step 3

00:05:34.572 --> 00:05:37.922
Check the Allocation is −$12, then save.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:39.964
Check your result

00:05:40.264 --> 00:05:43.795
The Record is complete, and the lunch Statement leaves Allocator.

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:45.625
Watch out

00:05:45.925 --> 00:05:51.815
If the Record says Pending, compare its amount with its Allocations, and fix whichever is wrong.

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:53.526
Example

00:05:53.826 --> 00:05:59.936
−$12 from the bank, −$12 allocated, a −$12 Lunch.

00:06:00.116 --> 00:06:02.359
Nothing remaining, and no difference.

00:06:03.967 --> 00:06:05.224
Protecting your work

00:06:05.824 --> 00:06:06.755
The problem

00:06:07.055 --> 00:06:09.208
Your data is saved in this browser.

00:06:09.388 --> 00:06:13.055
Clearing browser storage, or changing devices, can lose it.

00:06:13.933 --> 00:06:14.855
The idea

00:06:15.155 --> 00:06:19.962
A Finpoint backup is a separate copy of your data, saved as a JSON file.

00:06:20.142 --> 00:06:21.661
The browser downloads it.

00:06:21.841 --> 00:06:25.690
Requesting a download isn’t proof, so find the file yourself.

00:06:26.567 --> 00:06:27.499
Step 1

00:06:27.799 --> 00:06:29.791
In Sync, choose Download backup.

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:31.620
Step 2

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.376
Find the file, and keep it somewhere safe.

00:06:35.233 --> 00:06:36.205
Step 3

00:06:36.505 --> 00:06:39.256
Keep an older backup before you restore anything.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.297
Check your result

00:06:41.597 --> 00:06:43.143
You’ve found the backup file.

00:06:43.323 --> 00:06:45.802
Clicking the button alone doesn’t confirm that.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:47.625
Watch out

00:06:47.925 --> 00:06:51.660
Never clear browser storage until you have a backup you’ve checked.

00:06:52.533 --> 00:06:53.393
Example

00:06:53.693 --> 00:06:56.825
After explaining Lunch, Alex exports a backup.

00:06:57.005 --> 00:07:00.035
Restoring it later replaces what’s in this browser.

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:03.071
Splitting one payment

00:07:03.671 --> 00:07:04.602
The problem

00:07:04.902 --> 00:07:11.807
One −$80 supermarket Statement was really $60 of groceries and a $20 gift.

00:07:11.987 --> 00:07:15.048
Put it all in one Category, and that difference hides.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.821
The idea

00:07:17.121 --> 00:07:20.851
Create two Records, and allocate part of the Statement to each.

00:07:21.031 --> 00:07:24.690
The bank row stays whole, and you get two clear explanations.

00:07:25.567 --> 00:07:26.499
Step 1

00:07:26.799 --> 00:07:31.693
Create Groceries for −$60, from the −$80 Statement.

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:33.520
Step 2

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.754
−$20 is left.

00:07:35.934 --> 00:07:39.173
Create Gift for −$20, and allocate it.

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:41.005
Step 3

00:07:41.305 --> 00:07:44.524
Check both Records, and that $0 remains.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.564
Check your result

00:07:46.864 --> 00:07:51.795
−$60 plus −$20 equals −$80.

00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:55.439
Both Records are complete, and the Statement is fully used.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.259
Watch out

00:07:57.559 --> 00:08:00.241
You can’t allocate more than a Statement has left.

00:08:00.421 --> 00:08:01.804
Fix the Allocations;

00:08:01.984 --> 00:08:03.566
don’t invent bank rows.

00:08:04.433 --> 00:08:05.293
Example

00:08:05.593 --> 00:08:13.111
One Statement of −$80 becomes Groceries −$60 and Gift −$20.

00:08:13.291 --> 00:08:16.039
One bank row, two useful explanations.

00:08:17.667 --> 00:08:19.403
A shared dinner and repayment

00:08:20.003 --> 00:08:20.934
The problem

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:23.449
Alex pays $90 for dinner.

00:08:23.629 --> 00:08:26.891
Later, Alex receives $60 back from friends.

00:08:27.071 --> 00:08:30.620
Alex’s own cost is $30, not $90.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:32.422
The idea

00:08:32.722 --> 00:08:36.580
One Shared dinner Record can combine the payment and the repayment.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.383
The money back isn’t income.

00:08:38.563 --> 00:08:40.307
It reduces Alex’s share.

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:42.099
Step 1

00:08:42.399 --> 00:08:44.950
Create Shared dinner for −$30.

00:08:45.833 --> 00:08:46.786
Step 2

00:08:47.086 --> 00:08:50.164
Allocate −$90 from the dinner payment.

00:08:51.033 --> 00:08:52.005
Step 3

00:08:52.305 --> 00:08:54.912
Allocate +$60 from the repayment.

00:08:55.767 --> 00:08:56.931
Check your result

00:08:57.231 --> 00:09:01.786
−$90 + $60 = −$30.

00:09:01.966 --> 00:09:05.364
Both Statements are explained, and the Record is complete.

00:09:06.233 --> 00:09:07.192
Watch out

00:09:07.492 --> 00:09:10.842
Before the repayment arrives, the Record may show Pending.

00:09:11.022 --> 00:09:13.080
That alone doesn’t mean someone owes you.

00:09:13.933 --> 00:09:14.793
Example

00:09:15.093 --> 00:09:21.262
Payment −$90 and repayment +$60 go into one Shared dinner Record.

00:09:21.442 --> 00:09:24.040
It shows Alex’s −$30 share.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:27.842
Refunds, transfers and savings

00:09:28.442 --> 00:09:29.373
The problem

00:09:29.673 --> 00:09:35.825
A plus or minus sign doesn’t tell you whether money was income, spending, savings or a transfer.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:37.622
The idea

00:09:37.922 --> 00:09:40.715
Treatment tells the Dashboard how to count a Record.

00:09:40.895 --> 00:09:42.360
Income is for salary.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.254
Spending covers purchases and their refunds.

00:09:45.434 --> 00:09:48.870
Saving or investment is money to and from savings.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:52.913
Transfer or neutral is money moving between your own Accounts.

00:09:53.093 --> 00:09:55.109
Automatic just follows the sign.

00:09:55.967 --> 00:09:56.899
Step 1

00:09:57.199 --> 00:10:01.178
Use Income for salary, and Spending for purchases and refunds.

00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:02.986
Step 2

00:10:03.286 --> 00:10:12.221
For a transfer, combine the −$200 and +$200 into one $0 Record, as Transfer or neutral.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.072
Step 3

00:10:14.372 --> 00:10:18.311
Use Saving or investment for contributions and withdrawals.

00:10:19.167 --> 00:10:20.331
Check your result

00:10:20.631 --> 00:10:23.873
A +$20 refund lowers your net spending.

00:10:24.053 --> 00:10:27.227
Savings contributions show as positive amounts.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:29.059
Watch out

00:10:29.359 --> 00:10:35.458
A −$250 saving net means more cash went into savings than came out.

00:10:35.638 --> 00:10:37.401
It isn’t an investment loss.

00:10:38.267 --> 00:10:39.126
Example

00:10:39.426 --> 00:10:42.805
Put in $300 and take out $50:

00:10:42.985 --> 00:10:49.561
contributions $300, withdrawals $50, net −$250.

00:10:49.741 --> 00:10:53.110
The +$3,000 salary is separate income.

00:10:54.733 --> 00:10:56.170
Why a Record is Pending

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:57.701
The problem

00:10:58.001 --> 00:11:04.910
A Record is Pending when its amounts don’t match, its Allocations are missing, or one of its Statements is Pending.

00:11:05.767 --> 00:11:06.688
The idea

00:11:06.988 --> 00:11:10.698
A Pending Record is an explanation that doesn’t add up yet.

00:11:10.878 --> 00:11:15.712
A Pending Statement is a placeholder for bank activity you’re still waiting for.

00:11:15.892 --> 00:11:21.174
Even if the amounts match, the Record stays Pending until that placeholder is replaced.

00:11:22.033 --> 00:11:22.966
Step 1

00:11:23.266 --> 00:11:26.860
Open the Record and compare its amount with its Allocations.

00:11:27.733 --> 00:11:28.686
Step 2

00:11:28.986 --> 00:11:35.289
If lunch really cost $12, change the Record from −$10 to −$12.

00:11:36.167 --> 00:11:37.139
Step 3

00:11:37.439 --> 00:11:42.916
When the real bank row arrives, replace the Pending Statement instead of allocating twice.

00:11:43.767 --> 00:11:44.931
Check your result

00:11:45.231 --> 00:11:51.381
There’s at least one Allocation, the total matches the Record, and no allocated Statement is Pending.

00:11:52.233 --> 00:11:53.192
Watch out

00:11:53.492 --> 00:11:55.522
Fix the facts, not just the label.

00:11:55.702 --> 00:11:57.700
A Pending Record is still saved.

00:11:58.567 --> 00:11:59.426
Example

00:11:59.726 --> 00:12:04.705
The Record says −$10, but the Allocation is −$12.

00:12:04.885 --> 00:12:09.871
Change the Record to −$12, and the difference drops to $0.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.204
Finding and fixing mistakes

00:12:13.804 --> 00:12:14.735
The problem

00:12:15.035 --> 00:12:17.268
Something you saved seems to be missing.

00:12:17.448 --> 00:12:20.057
Usually filters or the month are hiding it.

00:12:20.237 --> 00:12:22.913
An empty list doesn’t mean your data is gone.

00:12:23.767 --> 00:12:24.688
The idea

00:12:24.988 --> 00:12:26.865
Records show your explanations.

00:12:27.045 --> 00:12:28.997
Statements show every bank row.

00:12:29.177 --> 00:12:31.913
Allocator only shows amounts left to explain.

00:12:32.093 --> 00:12:34.850
Use search and filters to narrow the right list.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:36.633
Step 1

00:12:36.933 --> 00:12:39.835
Check the month and date range, and clear filters.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:41.653
Step 2

00:12:41.953 --> 00:12:45.191
Look in Statements for rows Allocator no longer shows.

00:12:46.067 --> 00:12:47.039
Step 3

00:12:47.339 --> 00:12:50.602
Open the item, edit it, then check its totals again.

00:12:51.467 --> 00:12:52.631
Check your result

00:12:52.931 --> 00:12:56.838
The fixed item shows up where you expect, still linked to its bank row.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:58.659
Watch out

00:12:58.959 --> 00:13:01.028
Renaming doesn’t fix a wrong amount.

00:13:01.208 --> 00:13:03.354
Keep a backup before big changes.

00:13:04.233 --> 00:13:05.093
Example

00:13:05.393 --> 00:13:09.315
To find payments over $50, choose Paid and type 50.

00:13:09.495 --> 00:13:11.070
No minus sign needed.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.731
Category, treatment and bucket

00:13:15.331 --> 00:13:16.262
The problem

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:18.760
Three settings describe every Record.

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.307
Mixing them up makes totals confusing.

00:13:22.167 --> 00:13:23.088
The idea

00:13:23.388 --> 00:13:25.094
Category is what it’s for.

00:13:25.274 --> 00:13:27.355
Treatment is how totals count it.

00:13:27.535 --> 00:13:31.473
A bucket is an optional planning group, like Daily or Recurring.

00:13:31.653 --> 00:13:35.334
A Category can set defaults, and a Record can override them.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.133
Step 1

00:13:37.433 --> 00:13:39.938
In Categories, create the labels you need.

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:41.753
Step 2

00:13:42.053 --> 00:13:45.222
Give each Category a treatment and a default bucket.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.072
Step 3

00:13:47.372 --> 00:13:50.585
On a Record, check whether those defaults were overridden.

00:13:51.467 --> 00:13:52.631
Check your result

00:13:52.931 --> 00:13:56.113
Lunch can be Food, Spending and Daily all at once:

00:13:56.293 --> 00:13:57.707
three different choices.

00:13:58.567 --> 00:13:59.525
Watch out

00:13:59.825 --> 00:14:04.701
Changing a Category doesn’t update Records whose choices were set by hand.

00:14:05.567 --> 00:14:06.426
Example

00:14:06.726 --> 00:14:07.503
Lunch:

00:14:07.683 --> 00:14:10.618
Category Food, treatment Spending, bucket Daily.

00:14:10.798 --> 00:14:13.721
Rent is Spending too, but in the Recurring bucket.

00:14:15.333 --> 00:14:16.938
Monthly bucket targets

00:14:17.538 --> 00:14:18.469
The problem

00:14:18.769 --> 00:14:22.870
You want to compare a month’s spending with a plan, without moving any money.

00:14:23.733 --> 00:14:24.655
The idea

00:14:24.955 --> 00:14:28.095
A target is a monthly comparison for a bucket.

00:14:28.275 --> 00:14:30.848
A default carries forward from the month you set it.

00:14:31.028 --> 00:14:33.566
A target for only one month overrides it.

00:14:33.746 --> 00:14:36.555
Targets never reserve cash or block spending.

00:14:37.433 --> 00:14:38.366
Step 1

00:14:38.666 --> 00:14:40.815
On the Dashboard, pick the month first.

00:14:41.667 --> 00:14:42.620
Step 2

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:45.886
Choose this month only, or from this month onward.

00:14:46.767 --> 00:14:47.739
Step 3

00:14:48.039 --> 00:14:50.397
Compare the bucket’s spending with its target.

00:14:51.267 --> 00:14:52.431
Check your result

00:14:52.731 --> 00:15:00.492
Daily spending of $127 against a $500 target leaves $373.

00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:02.226
Example

00:15:02.526 --> 00:15:06.400
A $500 default starts in October and carries on.

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:13.642
A $600 target just for October wins in October, and later months go back to $500.

00:15:15.267 --> 00:15:17.028
Budgets for trips and events

00:15:17.628 --> 00:15:18.559
The problem

00:15:18.859 --> 00:15:21.757
A trip can cross months and several categories.

00:15:21.937 --> 00:15:23.877
A monthly bucket doesn’t fit it.

00:15:24.733 --> 00:15:25.655
The idea

00:15:25.955 --> 00:15:30.464
A Budget has its own amount, its own dates, and its own attached Records.

00:15:30.644 --> 00:15:33.166
It’s a separate view from the Dashboard’s totals.

00:15:34.033 --> 00:15:34.966
Step 1

00:15:35.266 --> 00:15:38.182
In Budgets, create one with an amount and dates.

00:15:39.033 --> 00:15:39.986
Step 2

00:15:40.286 --> 00:15:44.530
Leave Automatic attach on, or turn it off to choose Records yourself.

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.372
Step 3

00:15:46.672 --> 00:15:49.685
After changing dates, review the attached Records.

00:15:50.567 --> 00:15:51.731
Check your result

00:15:52.031 --> 00:15:55.795
The trip’s Records are attached, and fall inside the Budget’s dates.

00:15:56.667 --> 00:15:57.625
Watch out

00:15:57.925 --> 00:16:01.212
A Budget total doesn’t have to match Dashboard spending.

00:16:02.067 --> 00:16:02.926
Example

00:16:03.226 --> 00:16:05.619
Use a Budget for a week-long holiday.

00:16:05.799 --> 00:16:08.250
Use the Travel bucket for the monthly view.

00:16:09.867 --> 00:16:11.105
Reading the Dashboard

00:16:11.705 --> 00:16:12.636
The problem

00:16:12.936 --> 00:16:17.889
The charts depend on the month and on your Records, so they won’t match your bank balances.

00:16:18.767 --> 00:16:19.688
The idea

00:16:19.988 --> 00:16:24.858
The Dashboard summarizes your Records by treatment, then by Category and bucket.

00:16:25.038 --> 00:16:28.452
Monthly Records shows the Records behind each number.

00:16:28.632 --> 00:16:31.759
Pending Records already count, so tidy them first.

00:16:32.633 --> 00:16:33.566
Step 1

00:16:33.866 --> 00:16:34.866
Pick the month.

00:16:35.733 --> 00:16:36.686
Step 2

00:16:36.986 --> 00:16:40.062
Read income, spending, refunds and surplus.

00:16:40.933 --> 00:16:41.905
Step 3

00:16:42.205 --> 00:16:44.899
Use Monthly Records to trace any surprise.

00:16:45.767 --> 00:16:46.931
Check your result

00:16:47.231 --> 00:16:49.256
Income is $3,000.

00:16:49.436 --> 00:16:57.786
Gross spending is $147, minus $20 of refunds, so net spending is $127.

00:16:57.966 --> 00:17:01.237
Surplus is $2,873.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:03.059
Watch out

00:17:03.359 --> 00:17:07.305
If a number looks wrong, check the month first, then the Record’s treatment.

00:17:08.167 --> 00:17:09.026
Example

00:17:09.326 --> 00:17:13.788
A saving net of −$250 shows on its own line.

00:17:13.968 --> 00:17:16.926
It doesn’t mean income fell, or that you lost money.

00:17:18.533 --> 00:17:20.127
Backups and Google Drive

00:17:20.727 --> 00:17:21.658
The problem

00:17:21.958 --> 00:17:26.979
With two devices, you need a copy you can trust, and to know which copy is current.

00:17:27.833 --> 00:17:28.755
The idea

00:17:29.055 --> 00:17:30.734
Your data lives locally.

00:17:30.914 --> 00:17:33.045
A backup file is a separate copy.

00:17:33.225 --> 00:17:37.876
Google Drive sync is optional, and shares one workspace across devices.

00:17:38.056 --> 00:17:41.284
The sign-in broker never sees your financial data.

00:17:42.167 --> 00:17:43.099
Step 1

00:17:43.399 --> 00:17:46.189
In Sync, download a backup and find the file.

00:17:47.067 --> 00:17:48.020
Step 2

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:50.894
Connect Google Drive only if you want sync.

00:17:51.767 --> 00:17:52.739
Step 3

00:17:53.039 --> 00:17:56.945
If there’s a conflict, back up first, then choose which copy to keep.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:58.964
Check your result

00:17:59.264 --> 00:18:02.016
Up to date in Sync, and a backup you’ve found.

00:18:02.196 --> 00:18:03.169
Keep both.

00:18:04.033 --> 00:18:04.992
Watch out

00:18:05.292 --> 00:18:08.650
Don’t keep overwriting a conflict just to clear the warning.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:10.296
You choose a whole copy;

00:18:10.476 --> 00:18:11.822
nothing is merged.

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:13.560
Example

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:18.750
Your practice progress stays in this browser, without going into your backup or Drive.

00:18:20.367 --> 00:18:21.389
Your routine

00:18:21.989 --> 00:18:22.920
The problem

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.477
Don’t try to perfect everything at once.

00:18:25.657 --> 00:18:27.415
A short routine works better.

00:18:28.267 --> 00:18:29.188
The idea

00:18:29.488 --> 00:18:30.483
Each visit:

00:18:30.663 --> 00:18:37.897
import new activity, explain what’s left, inspect anything Pending, review the month, and protect it all with a backup.

00:18:38.767 --> 00:18:39.699
Step 1

00:18:39.999 --> 00:18:42.119
Import your latest bank CSV.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:43.953
Step 2

00:18:44.253 --> 00:18:46.353
Explain new amounts in Allocator.

00:18:47.233 --> 00:18:48.205
Step 3

00:18:48.505 --> 00:18:50.374
Review the month, then check Sync.

00:18:51.233 --> 00:18:52.397
Check your result

00:18:52.697 --> 00:18:57.720
You can follow any bank amount to its explanation, and get your work back if you need it.

00:18:58.600 --> 00:18:59.460
Example

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:05.569
Import, explain one purchase, fix one Pending Record, review the month, and check your backup.
